r and all three prediction markets put this race safely in Democratic hands, and the fundamentals agree: Hickenlooper combines a dominant fundraising edge, incumbency, and a Democratic-leaning state against a low-profile challenger with minimal resources. Minor-party candidate Bob Chew's $1.3 million haul is unlikely to change the two-party margin, pointing to a Democratic win in the mid-to-high teens.", "watch": "A sharply unfavorable national environment for Democrats late in the cycle, or a scandal touching the incumbent, is the main thing that could pull the margin toward single digits.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent John Hickenlooper (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Democratic Senator John Hickenlooper seeks a second term in a state that leans about six points Democratic, facing Republican challenger Mark Baisley and several minor-party candidates. Every major expert rating, ten in all, places the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets put Hickenlooper's chances at 98 to 99 percent. No public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on fundamentals.\n\nHickenlooper holds a decisive financial edge, having raised about $8.1 million to Baisley's roughly $140,000, and no outside groups are spending against him. Incumbency and Colorado's partisan lean both work in his favor despite the midterm environment. Minor-party candidate Bob Chew has raised more than $1 million but is unlikely to alter the two-party margin. This race sits comfortably in the Democratic column.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9917, "margin": 17.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9895, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9916, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.987, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9886, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9878, "margin": 16.5}, {"da